Before i-affection occurred, there was also a lowering of initial-syllable Proto-Celtic ''e'' to ''a'' before palatalized reflexes of {{lang|cel-x-proto|*g, *gʷ}}, unless a {{IPA|/j/}} followed them in the next syllable in Primitive Irish (no matter the {{IPA|/j/}}'s origin) which would instead lead to i-affection to ''i''. For instance, Proto-Celtic {{lang|cel-x-proto|*legeti-s}} "(s)he lies" vs. {{lang|cel-x-proto|*legonti-s}} "they lie" vs. {{lang|cel-x-proto|*legyom}} "lying" led to a three-way split in Old Irish {{lang|sga|laigid}}, {{lang|sga|legait}}, and {{lang|sga|lige}} respectively.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=111}}

