The result of {{lang|sga|i}}-affection and {{lang|sga|a}}-affection is that it is often impossible to distinguish whether the root vowel was originally {{lang|cel-x-proto|e}} or {{lang|cel-x-proto|i}} ({{lang|sga|sen}} < {{lang|cel-x-proto|senos}} and {{lang|sga|fer}} < {{lang|cel-x-proto|wiros}} have identical declensions). However, note the cases of {{lang|sga|nert}} vs. {{lang|sga|fiurt}} above for which {{lang|sga|i}}-affection, but not {{lang|sga|a}}-affection, was blocked by an intervening {{lang|sga|rt}}.

===Complications of u-infection===
The result of ''u''-infection of ''*a'' eventually reduced to /u/ during the Old Irish period. It does ''not'' share its later evolution with original ''*au'', which instead became {{lang|sga|ó}} (or broken into {{lang|sga|úa}}) in Old Irish.<ref name="aue" />

In addition, the u-affection of ''a'' when the ''u'' preceded a palatalized consonant originally turned the ''a'' into an {{IPA|lang=sga|/œ/}}, whose spelling varied among ''au'', ''ai'', ''i'', ''e'', and ''u'' depending on the scribe. {{IPA|lang=sga|/œ/}} then spread to various terms prefixed with {{lang|sga|ar-}} "fore-" and {{lang|sga|ad-}} "ad-".<ref name="aue" />

===Stressed front vowels in hiatus===
Stressed short front vowels in hiatus underwent a loop throughout Primitive Irish and early Old Irish in which they would repeatedly switch between ''i'' and ''e''. McCone outlines the loop as follows:<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|pages=109, 130}}

# Primitive Irish ''*e'' before a hiatus (i.e. over {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ɸ}} or ''*y'' between two vowels) raised to ''*i'' before a non-front vowel.
# Primitive Irish ''*i'' before a hiatus then underwent ''a''-affection normally, lowering it down to ''*e''.
# In early Old Irish, stressed ''*e'' before a hiatus raised again to ''i'' if the hiatus avoided syncope. If syncope had removed the hiatus, the ''e'' was not raised.

Examples of words that went through this loop include:
* The ''a''-subjunctive paradigm of {{lang|sga|crenaid}} "to buy". 
** The 3rd-person plural conjunct {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷreyasonti}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷriyad}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷread}} > {{lang|sga|·criat}} showcases a full loop.
** The 3rd-person plural relative {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷreyasontiyo}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷriyadiya}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷreade}} > {{lang|sga|crete}} shows Step 3 being skipped due to syncope deleting the hiatus that triggered the verb's progress in the loop.
* The declension of {{lang|sga|scé}} "whitethorn", where Step 1 is inapplicable.
** The nominative singular {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷiyats}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷiyah}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷeah}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷe}} > {{lang|sga|scé}} shows apocope destroying a hiatus, leading to Step 3 being skipped.
** The genitive singular {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷiyatos}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷiyaθah}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷeaθah}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷeaθ}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*skʷeaθ}} > {{lang|sga|sciad}} shows a completed cycle with Steps 2 and 3 undergone.

===Short vowels before nasals and stops===
Proto-Celtic ''*a'' before nasals followed by a stop manifested as the allophone {{IPA|[æ]}} in the prehistory of Irish.

Short vowels {{IPA|[æ]}} and ''*e'', and ''*i'' regularly became ''e'' before nasals followed by originally voiceless stops, which then lengthened to {{lang|sga|é}} in stressed syllables.
* {{lang|sga|cét}} "100" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kantom}} and {{lang|sga|éc}} "death" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ankus}}
* {{lang|sga|sét}} "path, way" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*sentus}}
* {{lang|sga|léicid}} "lets, leaves" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*linkʷeti-}} (secondarily transferred to the weak verbs)

But a different development of {{IPA|[æ]}} occurred before nasals followed by voiced stops. According to Schrijver, this {{IPA|[æ]}} became ''i'' when affected by ''i''-affection and ''a'' when it was not.<ref name="Schrijver 1991">{{cite journal | last=Schrijver | first=Peter | title=The Development of Primitive Irish aN before Voiced Stop | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=42 | year=1991 | jstor=30007533 | pages=13–25 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30007533 | access-date=May 31, 2022}}</ref> McCone however instead believes that ''i'' was the default outcome of {{IPA|[æ]}} before voiced nasals unless a-affection applied, lowering it down to ''a''.<ref name="McCone 1996"/> Some examples of these developments include:
* Without raising:
** {{lang|sga|land}} "land" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*landā}}
** {{lang|sga|camm}} "crooked" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kambos}}
** {{lang|sga|and}} "in it" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*andom}}
* With raising:
** {{lang|sga|bind}} "melodious" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*bandis}}
** {{lang|sga|imm}} "around" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ambi}}
** {{lang|sga|ingen}} "fingernail, toenail" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*angʷīnā}}
** {{lang|sga|imbliu}} "navel" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ambliyū}}

Additionally, {{IPA|[æ]}} and ''*e'' were also raised to ''i'' when followed by a nasal, a voiced stop, and then either ''*e'' or a word-final ''*a'' followed by a nasal, despite those vowels not triggering ''i''-affection.
* {{lang|sga|imb}} "butter" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*amban}}
* {{lang|sga|cing}} "he/she steps (conjunct)" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kenget}} < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kengeti}}

===Rounding of vowels by labiovelars===
In Primitive Irish, ''*a'' and ''*i'' were rounded to ''o'' and ''u'' respectively when preceded by Celtic labiovelars {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷ}} and {{lang|cel-x-proto|*gʷ}} or a [[consonant cluster]] containing them. The rounding of ''*i'' also required the following consonant to be palatalised. This rounding occurred after i-affection as {{lang|sga|·guid}} "prays" (< {{lang|cel-x-proto|*gʷedyeti}}) faced rounding even though the rounded vowel was originally an ''*e''.<ref name="Schrijver rounding">{{cite journal | last=Schrijver | first=Peter | title=Vowel Rounding by Primitive Irish Labiovelars | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=50 | year=1999 | issn=2009-0056 | jstor=30007731 | pages=133–137 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30007731 | access-date=September 19, 2022}}</ref> Since {{lang|sga|·cren}} "buys" (< {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷrinati}}) faced no rounding even though its stressed vowel was originally an ''*i'', the rounding may also have taken place after a-affection as well,<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=118}} but Schrijver does not find the evidence for that to be reliable.<ref name="Schrijver 1991"/>

Examples of this rounding process include:
* {{lang|sga|goin}} "wounds, kills" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*gʷaneti}}
* {{lang|sga|gor}} "pious" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*gʷaros}}
* {{lang|sga|coire}} "cauldron" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷaryos}}
* {{lang|sga|cruim}} "worm" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷrimis}}
* {{lang|sga|cruinn}} "of a tree" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷresnī}} (the nominative singular {{lang|sga|crann}} is secondary)
* {{lang|sga|cuit}} "portion" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷesdis}}

Original ''*a'' preceded by a labiovelar consonant and followed by ''*n'' and an originally voiceless stop was rounded and then broken into Old Irish {{lang|sga|óe, oí}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/oi̯/}}.<ref name="Schrijver 1993">{{cite journal | last=Schrijver | first=Peter | title=On the Development of Vowels Before Tautosyllabic Nasals in Primitive Irish | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=44 | year=1993 | issn=2009-0056 | jstor=30006876 | pages=33–52 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30006876 | access-date=September 24, 2022}}</ref>
* {{lang|sga|·góet}} "was wounded" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*gʷantos}}
* {{lang|sga|coíca}} "fifty" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷankʷkonts}}

