===Dissimilatory deletion of lenited consonants===
Lenited fricatives and {{IPA|/l/}} straddling the boundary between a stressed syllable and an unstressed one tend to disappear if there is a [[homorganic consonant]] near the end of the next syllable. If a non-front vowel comes into contact with a front vowel after it due to this deletion, the two vowels fuse into a diphthong like {{lang|sga|oí}} or {{lang|sga|aí}}. Otherwise a hiatus between the two vowels may be formed instead.<ref name="deac">{{cite journal | last=Schrijver | first=Peter | title=Varia IV. OIr. dëec, dëac | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=44 | year=1993 | issn=2009-0056 | jstor=30006888 | pages=181–184 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30006888 | access-date=June 25, 2022}}</ref>

For the purposes of this sound law, {{IPA|/h/}} is treated as if homorganic with ''s'', due to its general origin in lenitions of Proto-Celtic ''*s''.

Some examples of this sound law are given below:
* {{lang|sga|deac}}, {{lang|sga|deec}} {{IPA|/de.əɡ/}} "-teen" < ''*deǣg'' < ''*dexǣg'' < ''*dekank'' < ''*dekam-kʷe'' "and 10" ({{IPA|/x/}} deleted before {{IPA|/ɡ/}})
* {{lang|sga|oíd}} {{IPA|/oið/}} "lend!" < ''*oeðʲ'' < ''*oðeθʲ'' < ''*odete'' < ''*udete'' ({{IPA|/ð/}} deleted before {{IPA|/θ/}})
* {{lang|sga|coímthecht}} {{IPA|/koimʲ.θʲext/}} "company" < ''*koimbiθʲext'' < ''*koṽʲimbiθʲext'' < ''*kom-ambi-tixtā'' (lenited ''m'' deleted before non-leniting ''m'')
* {{lang|sga|foísam}} {{IPA|/foi.səṽ/}} "protection" < ''*foisaṽ'' < ''*fohissaṽ'' < ''*uɸo-sistamus'' (lenited ''s'' deleted before another ''s'')
* {{lang|sga|for·roíchan}} {{IPA|/forˈRoixən/}} "I taught" < ''*-rochechan'' < ''*-ɸro-kekana'' ({{IPA|/x/}} deleted before another {{IPA|/x/}})
* {{lang|sga|·fóelus}} {{IPA|/foilus/}} "I will sustain (prototonic)" < ''*folilussū'' < ''*uɸo-liluxsū'' ({{IPA|/l/}} deleted before another {{IPA|/l/}})

This deletion and diphthong formation happened before syncope.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=124}} As a demonstration, {{lang|sga|coímthecht}} ended up syncopating its etymon's third syllable instead of the usual second. This can be explained by the first and second syllables fusing into one syllable due to dissimilatory deletion, making the etymon's third syllable the second syllable at the time of syncope.

=== Intervocalic clusters ===
Many intervocalic clusters are reduced, becoming either a geminate consonant or a simple consonant with [[compensatory lengthening]] of the previous vowel. During the Old Irish period, geminates are reduced to simple consonants, occurring earliest when adjacent to a consonant. By the end of the Old Irish period, written {{lang|sga|ll mm nn rr}} are repurposed to indicate the non-lenited sounds {{IPA|/L m N R/}} when occurring after a vowel and not before a consonant.

Cluster reduction involving {{lang|cel-x-proto|n}}:
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|nt}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|nk}} > unlenited {{IPA|/d ɡ/}} (normally written {{lang|sga|t c}}). Note that PCelt {{lang|cel-x-proto|ant}},{{lang|cel-x-proto|ent}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|ent}} > {{IPA|/eːd/}} but {{lang|cel-x-proto|int}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|ont}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|unt}} > {{IPA|/idd odd udd/}} like {{lang|cel-x-proto|nk}}: {{lang|sga|cét}} {{IPA|/kʲeːd/}} "hundred" < PCelt {{lang|cel-x-proto|kantom}} (cf. [[Welsh language|Welsh]] {{lang|cy|cant}}) < PIE {{lang|cel-x-proto|kṃtóm}}; {{lang|sga|sét}} {{IPA|/sʲeːd/}} "way" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|sentu-}} (vs. [[Breton language|Breton]] {{lang|br|hent}}); {{lang|sga|ro·icc}}, {{lang|sga|ric(c)}} {{IPA|/r(o)-iɡɡ/}} "he reaches" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|ro-ink-}} (vs. Bret {{lang|br|rankout}} "must, owe"); {{lang|sga|tocad}} {{IPA|/toɡəð/}} "luck" (vs. Bret {{lang|br|tonkad}} "fate").<ref name="GOI"/>{{rp|pages=126–127}}
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|ns}} > unlenited {{lang|sga|s}} with [[compensatory lengthening]] of a preceding vowel; {{lang|cel-x-proto|ans}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|ens}} > {{lang|sga|és}} similarly to {{lang|cel-x-proto|ant}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|ank}}: {{lang|sga|géis}} "swan" < PCelt {{lang|cel-x-proto|gansi-}} < PIE {{lang|pgl|*ǵʰh₂ens-}} (vs. Dutch {{lang|nl|gans}} "goose").

Cluster reduction involving {{lang|cel-x-proto|s}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|z}}:
* Medial {{lang|cel-x-proto|sm}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|sn}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|sl}} > {{lang|sga|mm nn ll}}: {{lang|sga|am(m)}} "I am" < PIE {{lang|cel-x-proto|esmi}}.
* Medially, {{lang|cel-x-proto|st}} > {{lang|sga|ss}} (but {{lang|cel-x-proto|str}} > {{lang|sga|str}}, {{lang|cel-x-proto|rst}} > {{lang|sga|rt}}).
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|zb}} > {{lang|sga|db}} {{IPA|/ðb/}}, {{lang|cel-x-proto|zg}} > {{lang|sga|dg}} {{IPA|/ðɡ/}} (but {{lang|sga|rg}} after an unstressed syllable), {{lang|cel-x-proto|zd}} > {{IPA|/dd/}}: {{lang|sga|net}} {{IPA|/nedd/}} "nest" < PIE {{lang|cel-x-proto|nisdos}} {{IPA|/nizdos/}}.

Lenited stops {{lang|cel-x-proto|x}} {{lang|pgl|*ɣ}} {{lang|pgl|*θ|italic=yes}} {{lang|pgl|*ð}} generally disappear before [[sonorant]]s {{lang|cel-x-proto|r}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|l}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|n}} {{lang|cel-x-proto|m}}, with [[compensatory lengthening]] of the preceding vowel. Many examples occur in reduplicated preterites or words with consonant-final prefixes (such as {{lang|sga|ad-}}):<ref name="GOI"/>{{rp|page=79}}
* {{lang|sga|du·air-chér}} "I have purchased" < *-xexr < PCelt *-kikra;
* {{lang|sga|·cúal(a)e}} "he heard" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|koxlowe}} < PCelt {{lang|cel-x-proto|kuklowe}};
* {{lang|sga|áram}} "number" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|að-rīm}};
* {{lang|sga|dál}} "assembly" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|daθl}} (cf. Old Welsh {{lang|owl|datl}}).

However, {{lang|pgl|*θr}}, {{lang|pgl|*βr}}, {{lang|pgl|*βl}} survive: {{lang|sga|críathraid}} "he perforates" < PCelt {{lang|cel-x-proto|krētrāti-s}}; {{lang|sga|gabur}} "goat" < PCelt {{lang|cel-x-proto|gabros}} (cf. Welsh {{lang|cy|gafr}}); {{lang|sga|mebul}} "shame" < PCelt {{lang|cel-x-proto|meblā}} (cf. Welsh {{lang|cy|mefl}}).

Any /h/ that ends up in an intervocalic consonant cluster for any reason triggers the devoicing of adjacent consonants in the cluster.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=130}}
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ambisuweti}} > ''*æmbihoweθ'' > ''*imbhoi'' > {{lang|sga|·impai}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/ˈimpi/}} "turns around"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*dīslondīti}} > ''*dīhlondīh'' > ''*dīhlndīh'' > {{lang|sga|·díltai}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/ˈdiːlti/}} "denies"

===Reduction of {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-ssiy-}}===
Sequences of {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-ssiy-}} in Primitive Irish are reduced to simple ''*-ss-'' if the vowel preceding the sequence is unstressed.<ref name="McCone 2006"/>{{rp|pages=266–267}}
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-ast yo}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-assyo}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-assiyo}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-asso}} > {{lang|sga|-as}} (3rd-person singular s-preterite relative suffix)
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*exs-ande-wissiyū}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*essandewissū}} > {{lang|sga|aisndís}} "relating, telling"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*esti yo}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*essiyo}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*esso}} > {{lang|sga|as}} "is" (relative)

===''*w''===
Proto-Celtic ''*w'' had a complex series of outcomes in Old Irish.<ref name="Kortlandt 2007"/>{{rp|pages=60–61}}<ref name="Uhlich 1995">{{cite journal | last=Uhlich | first=Jürgen | title=On the Fate of Intervocalic *-ṷ- in Old Irish, Especially between Neutral Vowels | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=46 | year=1995 | issn=2009-0056 | jstor=30007872 | pages=11–48 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30007872 | access-date=August 26, 2022}}</ref>
* Word-initially, ''*w'' simply underwent [[fortition]] to ''f''. For instance, {{lang|cel-x-proto|*wiros}} became {{lang|sga|fer}} "man".
* Immediately after some consonants, ''*w'' became /v/, spelled ''b''. After other consonants, it was deleted. Kortlandt and McCone disagree on which conditions governed transformation into ''b'' and deletion. Kortlandt believes that ''b'' appeared when a voiced lenited consonant preceded the ''*w'', and if ''*w'' came after consonants that were either unlenited or voiceless it was deleted.<ref name="Kortlandt 2007"/>{{rp|page=61}} Meanwhile, McCone instead believes that ''*w'' was deleted syllable-initially while ''*w'' was transformed into ''b'' when the ''*w'' is separated from a consonant immediately before it by a pre-Irish syllable boundary.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=103}}
** Examples of transformation of ''*w'' to ''b'':
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*widwā}} > {{lang|sga|fedb}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/fʲeðv/}} "widow"
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*selwā}} > {{lang|sga|selb}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/sʲelv/}} "possession"
** Examples of deletion include:
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ardwos}} > {{lang|sga|ard}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/ard/}} "high"
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷetwores}} > {{lang|sga|cethair}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/kʲeθərʲ/}} "four"
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*swesūr}} > {{lang|sga|siur}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/sʲiur/}} "sister"
* ''*w'' was lost between two unstressed vowels.
** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*katowes}} > {{lang|sga|cathae}} "battles"
** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*biwos}} "alive" in unstressed position > {{lang|sga|tigbae}} "surviving"
* Intervocalically after a stressed vowel, ''*w'' persisted for longer until early Old Irish, even outlasting many deleted final syllables and syncopated vowels.
** ''*w'' was affected by Greene's second and third palatalisations, which turned it into {{IPA|lang=sga|/i̯/}}. If this new {{IPA|lang=sga|/i̯/}} came into contact with a preceding vowel, a diphthong ending in ''i'' was formed.
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*owis}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*owʲih}} > {{lang|sga|oí}} "sheep"
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*nowan}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*nowen}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*nowʲ}} > {{lang|sga|noí}} "nine"
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*yuwantūts}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*owẽddūh}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*owʲduh}} > {{lang|sga|oítiu}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/*oi̯dʲu/}} "youth"
** Unpalatalised ''*w'' formed u-diphthongs with preceding stressed vowels.
*** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*biwos}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*biwah}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*bewah}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*bew}} > {{lang|sga|béo}} "living, alive"

===MacNeill's law===
'''MacNeill's law''' refers to a sound law before and during the Old Irish period causing the loss of lenition of ''n'' and ''l'' in final unstressed syllables even though they are etymologically expected to be lenited in that position. Newly word-final ''n'' and ''l'' became unlenited ''nn'' and ''ll'' when the unstressed syllables containing them began in ''r'', ''n'', ''l'', {{IPA|lang=sga|/v(ʲ)/}}, or {{IPA|lang=sga|/m(ʲ)/}}.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=MacNeill |first=John |date=1908 |title=Notes on the Distribution, History, Grammar, and Import of the Irish Ogham Inscriptions |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/25502768 |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Irish Academy. Section C: Archaeology, Celtic Studies, History, Linguistics, Literature |volume=27 |pages=329–370 |issn=0035-8991}}</ref>{{Reference page|page=347}}<ref name="Stuber n-stems">{{cite book | last=Stüber | first=Karin | title=The Historical Morphology of N-stems in Celtic | publisher=Department of Old Irish, National University of Ireland, Maynooth | series=Maynooth studies in Celtic linguistics | year=1998 | isbn=978-0-901519-54-2 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=IEliAAAAMAAJ | access-date=December 20, 2022 | page=}}</ref>{{rp|pages=39–44}} A vowel between the trigger consonant and the affected ''l'' or ''n'' must also be present for the law to apply.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=134}} However, MacNeill's law often failed to apply if this vowel had been inserted by [[anaptyxis]] in the first place instead of coming from an earlier Proto-Celtic vowel.<ref name="Stifter apple">{{cite journal | last=Stifter | first=David | title=An apple a day… | journal=Indogermanische Forschungen | publisher=Walter de Gruyter GmbH | volume=124 | issue=1 | year=2019 | issn=1613-0405 | doi=10.1515/if-2019-0006 | pages=171–218| s2cid=208141167 | url=https://mural.maynoothuniversity.ie/11708/1/Stifter |language=de}}</ref>{{Reference page|pages=181–82}}

