==Destruction of final syllables==
Proto-Celtic final syllables were often reduced or deleted by Old Irish times.

===Raising of unstressed ''-es-''===
Unstressed Proto-Celtic ''-es-'' became ''-is-'' early on if immediately followed by a vowel. The resulting ''-is-'' triggers i-affection on preceding stressed syllables as it evolved into ''*-ih-'' and then ''*-iy-'' in Primitive Irish before either remaining as ''-i'' or undergoing a-affection to ''-e'' by Old Irish. This raising of ''*-es-'' occurred before the early deletion of final ''*-i''.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|pages=99–100}} McCone lists the following examples of this raising:

* In second-person singular forms of verbs:
** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*beresi-}} "you (sg.) bear" > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*berisi(-)}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*berih(i-)}} > Old Irish {{lang|sga|biri}}, conjunct {{lang|sga|·bir}}
* In the declension of neuter s-stems:
** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*nemesa}} "heavens" > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*neṽisa}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*neṽiha}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*niṽiya}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*niṽeya}} > Old Irish {{lang|sga|nime}} {{ipa|lang=sga|/ˈniṽʲe/}}
** {{lang|cel-x-proto|*nemesi}} "heaven" (dative singular) > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*neṽisi}} > > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*neṽis}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*neṽih}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*niṽih}} > Old Irish {{lang|sga|nim}} {{ipa|lang=sga|/ˈniṽʲ/}}

===Deletion of final ''*-i''===
The absolute-conjunct distinction in Old Irish non-prefixed verbs is generally explained via the [[apocope]] of final ''*-i'' in multiple Indo-European primary person-number endings that ended in ''*-i''. This normally happens in the conjunct forms, while in the absolute forms the apocope was blocked due to a succeeding enclitic element.<ref name="McCone 2006">{{cite book | last=McCone | first=Kim | title=The Origins and Development of the Insular Celtic Verbal Complex | publisher=Department of Old Irish, National University of Ireland | series=Maynooth studies in Celtic linguistics | year=2006 | isbn=978-0-901519-46-7}}</ref>{{rp|pages=106–109}} Contrast:
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*bereti-}} > {{lang|sga|beirid}} "carries" (absolute form without apocope)
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*bereti}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*beret}} > {{lang|pgl|*bereθ}} > {{lang|pgl|*berʲeh}} > {{lang|sga|·beir}} (conjunct form with apocope)

The identification of the enclitic that was used to create Old Irish's absolute verb forms has been subject to controversy. At first, [[Warren Cowgill]] and [[Frederik Kortlandt]] supposed that the protective enclitic was a particle derived from {{lang|cel-x-proto|*est(i)}} "is". The current mainstream explanation, pioneered by [[Peter Schrijver]] in the 1990s, identifies this particle as derived from {{lang|ine-x-proto|*éti}} "beyond", cognate to [[Latin]] {{lang|la|et}} "and". [[Kim McCone]] on the other hand refuses to identify any specific particle responsible.

The environment of final ''i''-deletion is also controversial. McCone believes that all final ''*-i'' was lost by default, while Schrijver limits the apocope to just after ''*t'', ''*s'' and also ''*k''.<ref name="Schrijver apocope">{{cite journal | last=Schrijver | first=Peter | title=The Celtic Adverbs for 'Against' and 'With' and the Early Apocope of -I | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=45 | year=1994 | issn=2009-0056 | jstor=30007718 | pages=151–189 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30007718 | access-date=September 28, 2022}}</ref>

===Final-syllable syncope between coronal continuants===
After the lenition of post-vocalic consonants in unstressed syllables and the apocope of ''-i'', an early Primitive Irish syncope occurred to vowels between two dental fricatives or two rhotics in final unstressed syllables preceded by another unstressed syllable.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|pages=105–106}} For dental fricatives, the result of their collision due to the syncope was originally {{IPA|lang=sga|/t/}} but would then become {{IPA|lang=sga|/d/}} due to a later voicing in the same environment as the syncope.<ref name="McCone 1981">{{cite journal | last=McCone | first=Kim | title=Final /t/ to /d/ after Unstressed Vowels, and an Old Irish Sound Law | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy | volume=32 | year=1981 | issn=2009-0056 | jstor=30007453 | pages=29–44 | url=http://www.jstor.org/stable/30007453 | access-date=December 25, 2022}}</ref> On the other hand, collision of two rhotics over this syncope would result in unlenited rhotic ''rr''. Unlike the main early Irish syncope, this syncope could ''never'' palatalise the resulting consonant produced by the collision of involved continuants, no matter what vowel was between them. Instances of this syncope include:

* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*toberetor}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*toberor}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*toverr}} > {{lang|sga|·tabarr}} "is brought/given" (prototonic)
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ɸare-nigʷedyeti}} > ''{{lang|cel-x-proto|*are-negʷeðiθ}}'' > ''{{lang|cel-x-proto|*are-negʷeðθ}}'' > {{lang|sga|ar·neget}} "(s)he prays"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-reteti}} > ''{{lang|cel-x-proto|*-reθeθ}}'' > ''{{lang|cel-x-proto|*-reθθ}}'' > {{lang|sga|-ret, -rat}} "runs" (when two or more prefixes come between the stressed syllable of a derived verb and the verb root)

===General final-syllable reductions in Primitive Irish===
McCone envisions the evolution of final syllables across Primitive Irish into Old Irish as follows.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|pages=120–121}}

Proto-Celtic unstressed long vowels were shortened unless protected by a following [[Primitive Irish]] final ''*-h'', whether that ''*-h'' came from a final ''-s'' (as in several nominal inflectional endings) or ''-ti'' (as in 3rd-person singular present forms of verbs). These shortened long vowels included the feminine ''ā''-stem nominative singular ending ''*-ā'' and the masculine and neuter ''o''-stem ending ''*-ī''.

Then, absolutely word-final ''-h'', vowels, and nasals caused initial mutations if possible, possibly by resegmentation onto the following word. Any remaining final-syllable short vowels, ''-h'', and nasals after this stage are deleted, while remaining final long vowels (which were subsequently shortened in Old Irish) remained. It is also apparent in Ogham inscriptions that final-syllable Proto-Celtic ''*o'' had become ''*a'' by Primitive Irish before its loss.

