===Anaptyxis before final sonorants===
After the general final-syllable deletion processes, newly word-final consonant clusters ending in sonorant consonants like liquids and nasals in early Irish that were not simplified with compensatory lengthening gained a [[schwa]] between the sonorant and the rest of the cluster. The schwa was often rendered with rounded vowel letters like ''o'' or ''u'' in early texts if next to a labial consonant.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=127}} For instance:

* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*aratrom}} > ''{{lang|pgl|*araθran}}'' > ''{{lang|pgl|*araθr}}'' > {{lang|sga|arathar}} "plough"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*brētrā}} > ''{{lang|pgl|*brēθra}}'' > ''{{lang|pgl|*brēθr}}'' > {{lang|sga|bríathar}} "word"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*dubnos}} > ''{{lang|pgl|*duvnah}}'' > ''{{lang|pgl|*dovn}}'' > {{lang|sga|domun}} "world"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*adkubrom}} > ''{{lang|pgl|*akkuvran}}'' > ''{{lang|pgl|*akkovr}}'' > {{lang|sga|accobor}} "wish, desire"

===Voicing of obstruents in unstressed syllables <span class="anchor" id="McCone's law"></span><span class="anchor" id="McCone's"></span>===
Voiceless obstruents, including voiceless stops and voiceless fricatives, were often voiced word-initially and word-finally in unstressed syllables. Each type of voiceless obstruent however had different triggers for them being voiced. These voicings occurred around AD 700.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|132–134}} These voicings are collectively labelled '''McCone's law''' by David Stifter after their main formulator Kim McCone.<ref name="Stifter 2017">{{cite book |last=Stifter |first=David |title=Handbook of Comparative and Historical Indo-European Linguistics |date=October 23, 2017 |publisher=De Gruyter |isbn=9783110523874 |pages=1188–1202 |chapter=68. The phonology of Celtic |doi=10.1515/9783110523874-023}}</ref>

====Dental obstruent voicing====
Dental obstruents {{IPA|lang=sga|/t, θ/}} were voiced in word-initial and word-final unstressed syllables, in addition to between two unstressed syllables. This wave of voicing is also believed to underlie the voicing of ''t-'' to ''d-'' in proclitics.<ref name="McCone 1981"/>
* {{lang|sga|ro·slogeth}} > {{lang|sga|ro·slogad}} "has been swallowed"
* {{lang|sga|díltuth}} > {{lang|sga|díltud}} "denial"
* {{lang|sga|·comalnathar}} varied with {{lang|sga|·comalnadar}} "fulfils" (conjunct)

====Voicing of ''f'' to {{IPA|lang=sga|/v/}}====
Happening along with the voicing of dental obstruents was the voicing of ''f'' to {{IPA|lang=sga|/v/}}, spelled ''b''. The voicing of ''f'' has been believed to occur in near-identical environments to the voicing of dentals. Schrijver and McCone think the voicing of ''f'' could also happen word-finally in stressed syllables as well, but Stifter is not confident in the evidence.<ref name="Stifter apple"/>
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*weswā}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*wefa}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*wef}} > {{lang|sga|feb}} "excellence" (but Stifter thinks it may have spread from its use as a conjunction)
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*swesros}} > {{lang|sga|for, far}} varying with {{lang|sga|bar, bor}} "your (pl.)"

====Voicing of {{IPA|lang=sga|/xʲ/}}====
Palatalised voiceless velar fricatives {{IPA|lang=sga|/xʲ/}} became their voiced counterparts {{IPA|lang=sga|/ɣʲ/}} word-finally and between unstressed vowels.
* {{lang|sga|dásachtaich}} > {{lang|sga|dásachtaig}} "madmen" (nominative singular {{lang|sga|dásachtach}})
* {{lang|sga|sóinmiche}} > {{lang|sga|sóinmige}} "prosperity"

This voicing of palatalised velar fricatives created paradigmatic alternations in noun declension where final unpalatalised voiceless velar fricatives alternated with palatalised voiced velar fricatives. Occasionally, nouns originally ending in non-palatal {{IPA|lang=sga|/ɣ/}} in their paradigms had those unpalatalised voiced velar fricatives analogically devoiced to {{IPA|lang=sga|/x/}}, for instance in {{lang|sga|tech}} < {{lang|sga|teg}} < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*tegos}} "house".

===Loss of intervocalic ''-s-''===
Intervocalic single Proto-Celtic ''*-s-'' was lenited to ''-h-'' and then lost in the following manners:<ref name="aue">{{cite journal | last=Qiu | first=Fangzhe | title=Old Irish aue 'descendant' and its descendants | journal=Indogermanische Forschungen | publisher=Walter de Gruyter GmbH | volume=124 | issue=1 | date=September 18, 2019 | issn=1613-0405 | doi=10.1515/if-2019-0013 | pages=343–374| hdl=10197/12242 | s2cid=208141257 | url=https://mural.maynoothuniversity.ie/12899/1/Qiu%20aue%20descendant.pdf }}</ref>
* Usually the ''-h-'' is lost entirely, creating a hiatus where it once was. For instance, {{lang|cel-x-proto|*woseti}} "to stay (somewhere)" became {{lang|sga|foïd}} "to stay the night", a hiatus verb.
* After an ''*i'' or an original diphthong ''*ai'' or ''*oi'', the ''-h-'' became a glide ''-y-'' before its loss. Qiu gives {{lang|cel-x-proto|*tegesa > *teɣiha > *tiɣeya}} > {{lang|sga|tige}} "houses" as an example. Earlier, McCone gives {{lang|sga|senchae}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/sʲen.xe/}} "seer" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*seno-xʷoya < *seno-xʷoih(y)ah < *seno-kʷois-(y)os}} (literally "old-seer") as a demonstration of this.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=132}}

===Changes to vowels surrounding former intervocalic ''-s-'' or glides===
Vowels surrounding a former intervocalic ''-s-'', a glide ''*w'' or ''*y'', or both underwent special changes by Old Irish.

In unstressed syllables, such vowels generally fused into /e/.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|pages=131–132}}
* {{lang|sga|·rabae}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/Rave/}} "has been" (perfect prototonic) < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ɸro-(be)buwe}}
* {{lang|sga|senchae}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/sʲen.xe/}} "seer" < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*seno-kʷois-(y)os}} (literally "old-seer")
* {{lang|sga|-mae}} (1pl. relative ending) < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*-mosiyo}}
* {{lang|sga|berae}} {{IPA|lang=sga|/bʲe.re/}} "bear" (2sg. subjunctive) < {{lang|cel-x-proto|*berasesi}}

==Changes to proclitics==
[[Proclitic]]s that precede a stressed syllable undergo special sound changes during the Old Irish period.

===Initial consonants===
Initial ''s'' was deleted in proclitics.<ref name="GOI"/>{{rp|111}}
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*sindos}} > {{lang|sga|in(t)}} "the" (masculine nominative singular)
* {{lang|sga|samail}} "likeliness" (dative singular) became {{lang|sga|ama(i)l}} when used as a preposition or conjunction in pretonic position.

Initial ''t'' in pretonic position was voiced to ''d-''.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|pages=132–133}}
* {{lang|sga|to-}} (a verb-forming prefix) became {{lang|sga|do-}} in pretonic position.
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*towe}} > {{lang|sga|do}} "your (sg.), thy"

===Vowels===
Long vowels merged with their corresponding short vowels in proclitics.<ref name="GOI"/>{{rp|33}}
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*dū}} > {{lang|sga|du}} "to, for"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kʷākʷos}} > {{lang|sga|cach}} "each, every" (proclitic counterpart of {{lang|sga|cách}} "everyone", which retained the long vowel)

This was followed by ''o'' merging with ''u'', with the resulting vowel spelled with both ''u'' and ''o'' in Old Irish.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=135}}
* {{lang|sga|du}} > {{lang|sga|do, du}} "to, for"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*nu}} "now" > {{lang|sga|nu, no}} (dummy preverb)
* {{lang|sga|to-}} > {{lang|sga|do-}} > {{lang|sga|do-, du-}} (a verb-forming prefix in deuterotonic verbs)
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|ɸro-}} > {{lang|sga|ro-, ru-}} (verbal augmentation prefix)

At the same time, ''e'' became ''a'' in proclitics, except for ''e'' before ''*nt'' which instead merged with ''i'' with the spelling vacillating between ''e'' and ''i''.<ref name="Lash 2017">{{cite journal |first=Elliott|last=Lash| title=A quantitative analysis of ''e/i'' variation in Old Irish ''etar'' and ''ceta'' | journal=Ériu | publisher=Royal Irish Academy| volume=67 | year=2017 | issn=0332-0758 | doi=10.3318/eriu.2017.67.3 |pages=141–167|s2cid=171844008 }}</ref>
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*esmi}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|*emmʲ}} > {{lang|sga|am}} "I am"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*exs-}} > {{lang|sga|ess-}} > {{lang|sga|as-}} "ex-" (in deuterotonic verbs)
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*ɸleti}} > {{lang|cel-x-proto|let}} > {{lang|sga|le}} > {{lang|sga|la}} "with"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*enter}} > {{lang|sga|eter, etar, itar}} "between"
* {{lang|cel-x-proto|*kentu-}} > {{lang|sga|ceta-, cita-}} "first"

===Depalatalisation===
Palatalisation was generally lost in proclitics.<ref name="McCone 1996"/>{{rp|page=135}}
* {{lang|sga|amail}} > {{lang|sga|amal}} "like, as"
* {{lang|sga|tochim}} > {{lang|sga|dochum}} "to, towards"

